    "story": "Still light in the window.  \nThe kind that lingers past its hour.\n\nThe house holds its breath.  \nFloorboards quiet.  \nThe air thinner than it was a moment ago.\n\nShe keeps her arms folded, eyes on the glass,  \nbracing for the shape she knows—  \nthe way you brace without anger,  \nlong enough to notice the light has changed.\n\nShe stays.  \nAs if the image might answer back.",
    "notes": "This piece distinguishes itself through restraint and psychological tension rather than narrative display. The composition relies on posture, interior geometry, and light as primary actors, placing it in dialogue with artists such as Andrew Wyeth and Edward Hopper, where stillness carries as much meaning as action.\n\nWayne Heim’s painterly approach softens edges and contrast, allowing atmosphere and texture to guide the eye instead of sharp detail. The result is an image that resists immediate consumption, rewarding extended viewing as subtle shifts in light and balance reveal themselves over time.\n\nWhat makes this work distinctive within contemporary Western art is its refusal of spectacle. Rather than depicting the mythic West through drama or nostalgia, the image focuses on vigilance, endurance, and interior resolve—qualities rooted in lived frontier experience. It is a work that deepens with familiarity, offering collectors a piece that continues to reveal itself rather than declare itself.",
