    "story": "Counted three riders leaving, two stragglers left behind.  \nSun’s dropping, beans still in the pot—nobody hungry yet, but they will be.\n\nNothing much to do now except listen for laughter,  \nwatch the shadows get long,  \nand wonder if there’s enough salt for what’s coming.\n\nIt’s always the waiting that tastes the strongest,  \nlong before the supper bell ever rings.",
    "notes": "“Waiting on Supper” is a portrait of the unsung anchor of camp life—the cowboy cook—crafted with the patient, watchful presence that defined Russell Lee’s Depression-era photography and the painterly gravitas of William Clift. The composition is all centered weight and subtle command: the cook’s posture, apron, and boots ground the image, while the softened background frames him as both participant and quiet observer.\n\nTechnically, the photograph embraces painterly monochrome and gentle backlighting to reveal every fold of cloth and weathered line of experience. The chef’s gaze is focused outward, beyond the viewer, capturing a world of stories half-heard and judgments unspoken.  \n\nThis image is for collectors who appreciate narrative restraint, dignity in routine, and the enduring wisdom of those who keep the fire lit—one meal, and one story, at a time.",
