    "story": "Go on, judge the hat, the smile, the shine on my boots—  \neveryone’s looking for a reason to believe or to doubt.\n\nAll it takes is a wink, a whispered promise,  \nand the crowd leans closer, wanting to be sold hope  \nthey already know won’t last.\n\nBottle in hand, heart in my throat—  \nI’m just another act on the frontier stage,  \nwaiting to see if tonight the magic is real,  \nor if I’ll need to pack up before the sun is high.",
    "notes": "“Step Right Up” is a portrait that revels in the theatricality and sly charisma of the classic Western pitchman, calling to mind the stagey bravado of early vaudeville and the sly glances immortalized in the photographs of Mike Disfarmer and circus-era Irving Penn. Every detail is deliberate: bowler hat at a jaunty angle, vest buttoned tight, eyes never quite letting you off the hook.\n\nThe composition frames the salesman as both performer and outsider—foregrounded, sharp, and cocked half in invitation, half in challenge. Painterly monochrome softens the backdrop but leaves the character’s features sharp, coaxing humor and suspicion in equal measure.  \n\nCollectors will appreciate the image’s narrative wit and technical polish—a modern Western tableau that exposes both the hope and hustle at the heart of the frontier. It’s a wink, a dare, and a bit of history all in one bottle.",
