    "story": "Steam curls off the coffee,  \nsun barely over the rooftops, boots still cold from the night.\n\nNot much gets said—  \njust the quiet scrape of tin,  \nthe slow way the day slips in around the edges.\n\nHe wonders what’ll happen first—  \ntrouble riding in,  \nor the warmth finally reaching his hands.  \nEither way, it’s good to have company.",
    "notes": "“Morning Watch” captures the understated poetry of daily ritual on the frontier, echoing the documentary candor of Walker Evans and the still-life quietude of 19th-century tintype portraiture. The composition leans into symmetry and negative space: two cowboys, coffee mugs in hand, anchored against weathered wood and the washed-out promise of a new day.\n\nPainterly monochrome distills the scene to essential forms—steam, breath, soft light, the subtle dialogue between posture and gaze. Nothing is rushed; every detail supports the narrative of endurance, mutual respect, and the rare comfort of shared silence.  \n\nThis image will resonate with collectors who appreciate narrative restraint, mood, and the slow beauty of honest work—where the strongest bonds are forged not in words, but in the quiet minutes before the world wakes up.",
