    "story": "Don’t fidget.  \nLet them guess what you’re thinking.\n\nEvery glance—they’re giving something away.  \nNo one sees the hand I’m holding, not yet.\n\nIt’s just patience.  \nA little quiet, a little calculation,  \nand soon enough, the room will move the way I want.",
    "notes": "“The Quiet Calculation” distills the archetype of the Western tactician—a figure who shapes events not by force, but by anticipation. This portrait’s direct gaze and poised hand recall the reserved intensity of classic film antiheroes and the compositional control of Richard Avedon’s Western work. The subject’s posture—still, yet brimming with intent—invites the viewer to speculate on the moves yet to come.\n\nPainterly monochrome brings out the textural interplay of hat, collar, and skin, emphasizing the subtlety of thought over action. What’s left unsaid is as important as what’s visible; this is a study in reading the room, watching for tells, and maintaining control even when the world outside is uncertain.",
