    "story": "Sun’s dropped, but the grass still moves out there.  \nOld rifle feels heavier when there’s nothing to shoot at,  \nheavier still when you’ve outlasted the herds.\n\nToo much silence for anyone but the wind.  \nMemories drift in with the dusk—  \nfaces, stampedes, nights colder than this.\n\nHe sits the vigil because it’s habit,  \nbecause he’s not sure how to do anything else,  \nand because sometimes, a man’s only job is to wait.",
    "notes": "“Silent Prairie Vigil” is a meditation on endurance and memory—an image that turns the prairie’s emptiness into both subject and atmosphere. The hunter’s pose, equal parts rest and readiness, echoes the classic Western painters but with a modern, psychological touch: a portrait of a man who has outlasted the herds, still keeping watch for something that may never return.\n\nLight and composition are painterly, with subtle gradations softening the tension between figure and land. The empty space around him feels as important as the subject himself—much like the postwar works of Russell or the existential quiet of Ansel Adams’ human studies. Here, the rifle is less a weapon than an anchor, a reminder of vanished herds and uncounted miles.",
